<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Altyndepe</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 3200–2000 BCE</span> · Also known as Altyn Tepe, Altyn-Depe, Altyn Depe</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Altyndepe (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> for 'Golden Hill') is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> archaeological site of the BMAC near Aşgabat in Turkmenistan. It was inhabited from c. 3200 to 2400 BCE and from c. 2400 to 2000 BCE, as a full urban site; in the late Chalcolithic it had grown into a centre of 25 hectares surrounded by an adobe wall with rectangular watch towers. Its remains include a monumental religious complex with a four-level tower of the Mesopotamian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziggurat">ziggurat</a> type, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus-valley">Indus Valley</a> finds such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> objects and Harappan-type stamp seals. Models of two-wheeled carts from c. 3000 BCE found there are the earliest complete evidence of wheeled transport in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>. Large-scale excavations began in 1965.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/altyndepe.jpg" alt="Altyndepe" width="330" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Limin Huan · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Altyndepe,_Turkmenistan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
